From c35834a29fe3a86b542297ee34273b2d49182b60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:12:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] measure: commit timing is state-dependent, and no
 constant beats the fixed setpoint

Headroom measurement asked by T10 section 5, on the corrected lower layer, h35,
twelve seeds, scripted 2D action, no training and no checkpoints. All 44
episodes solve the QP on every step, so these are task-level outcomes rather
than solver artifacts.

The fixed setpoint completes 8/12. Choosing the commit time per seed reaches
11/12. Every constant commit time measured over all twelve is worse than the
fixed setpoint: T=95 gives 7/12, T=110 gives 6/12, and both break four of the
eight the fixed setpoint completes.

The twelve seeds split into two groups whose feasible commit sets are disjoint:
four complete only at T=0, three only at T>=95, four at both, one at neither.
262001's feasible set is a single grid point wide, which the first 30 s grid
missed entirely.

The extra completions are not taken out of the existing ones. On all eight
baseline-completing seeds the per-state choice picks T=0, so it is the fixed
setpoint there bitwise; mean fuel rises from 173.3 to 293.4 N s purely because
three episodes that finished nowhere now finish, at 406 to 765 N s each.

Stated as an oracle, not a controller: the commit time was chosen per seed
knowing the outcome, so this is an upper bound on what a state-dependent rule
could reach and must never appear as a main-table row. It establishes that the
rule depends on the state; it does not establish that the rule must be learned,
which is the next comparison rather than a step to skip. n=12 on a seven-point
grid; the 48-seed version still belongs to the lower window.

