From a5ce53b681ec340e65ce3062e4be2f0e4fc5bb73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:22:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] fix: judge the constant-commit question on the net, not
 on the rescue side

The first version flagged "a constant would do" whenever one commit time
rescued every rescuable seed. On this data that fires for T=95 -- and it is
wrong: T=95 also breaks four of the eight seeds the fixed setpoint completes,
so its net is 7/12 against the baseline's 8/12. A constant that rescues
everything and loses more elsewhere is not enough, and gate B would have been
read backwards.

The report now tabulates net completion per commit time against the baseline,
marks grid points that were not run on every seed as not comparable rather than
counting them, and only says a constant may suffice when some fully covered
commit time matches or beats the baseline. On the twelve-seed block it says the
opposite: no constant matches 8/12, and only the per-state choice reaches 11/12.

Also backfills the baseline as a real commit_now run over all twelve seeds, so
every artifact in logs/t10_headroom comes from one script. It reproduces
diagnose_terminal_gate on all eleven compared fields with no fuel difference
above 0.01 N s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KfdW4y9TEkhu2Sc1bKnkER
